e advice to be your  most authentic self  um just to pivot just a bit though i do  want to ask about you know gender  diversity in science and engineering um  we often talk about a leaky pipeline  referring to sort of the decreasing  proportion of women and underrepresented  minorities in stem i want to ask you  what you suggest graduate schools can do  to not only attract but also to retain  new generations of scientists  so i have been inspired to push back on  the concept of the leaky pipeline  because when we talk about a leaky  pipeline that's suggesting a passive  loss but in reality the pipeline that we  exist in has valves and they're  restricting access to some people based  on their identities and those identities  can be race sexuality gender physical  ability uh myriad things so  how do we  fix this pipeline how do we stop uh stop  this restriction of access so  i think it's uh really important to make  underrepresented students or team  members feel seen all of the time and  create an environment that is supportive  and collaborative for them  everyone should be encouraged to speak  and that they have an equal voice in the  team  i think the graduate school and all  programs should implement holistic  admissions uh processes that focus more  on research potential  by like qualitative assessments and  de-emphasize metrics like the gre and  gpa that are actually been proven to be  ineffective predictors for research  performance and degree completion uh  research has shown that letters of  recommendation are the strongest  predictor for success in graduate school  programs  so i also think we should make academic  faculty candidate lists that are diverse  we need to diversify the faculty because  that's going to aid in the retention and  recruitment of diverse students uh we  need to make sure that we're taking  serious looks at these diverse  candidates and deans and department  chairs should be rejecting short lists  that lack diversity we should i woul