vey so every 10 years um  people sit down and they think 10 years  out and the discussion is where what  space what science missions so like what  planets or bodies would they like to  visit in 10 years and they'll say oh  it'd be really cool if we could go to  you know europa or let's try to you know  set up camp on the moon and then from  that we divorced what technology do we  need to invent so that we can fulfill  this mission and that's where the  scientists like me you know the  engineers come in is to create this  technology such that we can conduct  those missions so we're kind of on  demand enabling um this technology and  then there's so many spin-off  technologies that we get from that you  know as we develop these things for  space we find a lot of practical uses  for them here on earth and then we spin  them out um again to companies that end  up boosting the economy and giving us  you know this great new technology um  that  probably would not have been discovered  if they didn't if we didn't have that  latitude and that freedom of imagination  to think so wonky and so far out of the  box and to be so creative  and i also love that nasa really values  people the environment in the community  so it's tremendously important to me as  a person that i am doing little to no  harm to our earth um and i appreciate  working at a place that's not polluting  you know not uh robbing resources and  not you know being a negative presence  on our planet um so i really appreciate  just the values of of our agency  this is great i i you know what i've  been hearing  um also is something that resonates  actually in other disciplines i feel  like  you know it goes back to that question  about the you know the single item stuff  versus the article i mean a lot of times  you want to think about the problem  and then think about the challenge in  general and think about what makes sense  just like what you do in  in biotechnology for example what does  the biology tell you