e  cool if and so we're we're a bit of the  flower child in that way but i think  we're really valuable in that sense  that's great thank you we actually  i have a a really really fun question  here from the colorado yale association  um  have you watched a new june movie and  you like science fiction fiction or  afrofuturism i love it by the way have i  watched which movie the new june  oh  yes i did watch that and it was fun i've  really been getting into um sci-fi  lately i've been reading it a bit more  uh than watching movies because that's a  bit more of my speed but yeah i enjoyed  that movie and i um i also love sci-fi  and i  get really excited by sci-fi they  they provide a lot of like information  and perspectives and kind of uh  situations that i hadn't considered yet  um and then i'm like is this could this  happen is this real like maybe we could  so then i'm amped up and i want to go  into the lab and try to make it happen  um so yeah i i'm a big fan  that's really cool yeah i'm a big fan of  the movie i i really just i i was on my  seat all the time and that was just wow  it was it was great um all right well i  don't know dan again do you think we we  have room maybe like one last question  maybe for one last question lindsay  thank you uh for that answer i i must  admit i do not know that popular  cultural reference so i'm gonna have to  catch up on dune it sounds like  something i should be into  um but just for one you know closing  question here it's a it's a big one but  i'm hoping that  you'll have a succinct answer  what is the one piece of advice you  would give to yale students in the  audience  oh that's a great one  i'm going to cheat and give one personal  advice and one professional advice um so  the personal advice i would tell yale  students is remember  that life goes on while you're in grad  school um so this is like four to seven  plus ten ish years that you're that  you're there but time isn't going to  stand still so nurture rela